WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.017
Contingency planning assumes weather, battery, communication, navigation, or recovery conditions may change.

00:00:06.017 --> 00:00:13.371
Identify safe landing zones, route decision points, minimum reserves, crew calls, and responsibilities before takeoff.

00:00:13.371 --> 00:00:17.159
Loss of signal requires a site-specific response.

00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:24.513
Review configured behavior, home point, return route, altitude assumptions, terrain, wind, obstacles, and recovery-area status.

00:00:24.513 --> 00:00:28.747
Return-to-home is a limited automation, not a guarantee.

00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:33.872
Rapid weather change may require an immediate return or landing.

00:00:33.872 --> 00:00:43.455
A strong headwind and low battery can make the planned home point less safe than a nearby approved emergency area.

00:00:43.455 --> 00:00:47.689
Preserve options through early thresholds and simple routes.

00:00:47.689 --> 00:00:55.043
Manual intervention may be required when automation makes an unsafe assumption or navigation becomes unreliable.

00:00:55.043 --> 00:01:04.179
Operators should act within training and procedure, communicate clearly, and prioritize people and property over the aircraft or data.

00:01:04.179 --> 00:01:15.099
After an abnormal event, secure the area, account for people and equipment, preserve logs and media, inspect the aircraft, and make required notifications.

00:01:15.099 --> 00:01:21.116
Do not relaunch until weather, equipment, authorization, and cause have been reviewed.

00:01:21.116 --> 00:01:28.024
Document what happened, what cues appeared, what decisions were made, and what should change.

00:01:28.024 --> 00:01:36.715
Lessons should update thresholds, route design, training, maintenance, and briefings without encouraging future crews to accept more risk.
